\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Fenced promotion preserves the single-writer invariant. Left (single-border): with fencing, the stale command is forced to rejoin as a responder. Right (double-border): without fencing, two command authorities can append concurrently, producing a forked journal. The unsafe branch appears as a 3-state TLA+ trace for split authority and a 5-state trace for a forked journal; durable epoch storage and stale-epoch rejection are implemented in the prototype.}}{5}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:promotion-fencing}{{6}{5}{Fenced promotion preserves the single-writer invariant. Left (single-border): with fencing, the stale command is forced to rejoin as a responder. Right (double-border): without fencing, two command authorities can append concurrently, producing a forked journal. The unsafe branch appears as a 3-state TLA+ trace for split authority and a 5-state trace for a forked journal; durable epoch storage and stale-epoch rejection are implemented in the prototype}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:promotion-fencing@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][5][]5}}
